    </openGraphImage>
    <pageContent>
      <Value><![CDATA[{
  "contentData": [
    {
      "contentTypeKey": "a58b29f0-29c6-47b8-b8ce-329b38fbed3c",
      "key": "791280db-4289-4a12-8380-8469ad80cbe0",
      "values": [
        {
          "alias": "text",
          "culture": null,
          "editorAlias": null,
          "segment": null,
          "value": {
            "blocks": {
              "contentData": [],
              "settingsData": [],
              "expose": [],
              "Layout": {}
            },
            "markup": "\u003Cdiv class=\u0022component-inner\u0022\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\u0022info\u0022\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\u0022text base-text\u0022 data-os-animation=\u0022fadeIn\u0022 data-os-animation-delay=\u00220s\u0022\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIt\u2019s hard to believe that we\u2019re nearly at the end of the year! 2024 has gone so quickly. We\u2019ve been very busy again, working on some great projects, continuing to support our amazing clients and sorting things out like our ISO 27001 accreditation. During that time, we\u2019ve also managed to squeeze in a few articles on this blog, covering aspects of working with an agency, digital customer experience, platforms like Umbraco and more.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn this post we look back at ten of our most popular articles that you may have missed during the year.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ca href=\u0022/3chillies-blog/ten-digital-customer-experience-trends-for-2024/\u0022 title=\u0022Ten digital customer experience trends for 2024\u0022\u003ETen digital customer experience trends for 2024\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ERight at the beginning of the year we looked at some of the trends in the digital customer experience space that we thought we\u2019d encounter in 2024. We covered everything from developments in the CMS and DXP space to increased cybersecurity threats to the widespread impact of generative AI. The big question is with the benefit of hindsight, did our predictions turn out to be right? Reading them now, they don\u2019t actually look too bad!\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ca href=\u0022/3chillies-blog/11-critical-boxes-to-tick-before-you-embark-on-a-website-project/\u0022 title=\u002211 critical boxes to tick before you embark on a website project\u0022\u003E11 critical boxes to tick before you embark on a website project\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWe sometimes get asked about what needs to be done to get ready for a new website project, or if the timing is right to embark on what can be a major investment. In this post from back in February we explored the ducks that ideally need to be in a row before embarking on a new website build. The post lists eleven points from deciding on the high-level strategy through to setting yourself up for good communication. We also cover RFIs, RFPs, making the business case, setting up the project team and more!\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ca href=\u0022/3chillies-blog/sitecore-update-eight-recent-developments/\u0022 title=\u0022Sitecore Update: Eight Recent Developments\u0022\u003ESitecore Update: eight recent updates\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOne of the strengths of Sitecore is that they keep on investing in the product, adding new capabilities and features, as well as expanding the overall platform. Over the past two years Sitecore has been on a journey that offers the core platform on a SaaS basis with separate products that can be integrated individually into a composable or best-of-breed ecosystem.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOver two posts we explored the latest updates from Sitecore. The first was from back in March with a new CEO and more product roadmap in changes. We followed this up in October with another update that detailed announcements from the Symposium conference, a new revenue milestone and the release of Sitecore Stream \u2013 a branded set of AI capabilities.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ca href=\u0022/3chillies-blog/what-to-expect-when-upgrading-your-content-management-system/\u0022 title=\u0022What to expect when upgrading your Content Management System\u0022\u003EWhat to expect when upgrading your Content Management System\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAll website teams will go through a CMS upgrade project from time to time. Generally, with the advent of SaaS and a move to more stable releases across many of the CMS and DXPs, they\u2019re usually pretty straightforward. However, they are often still viewed with some trepidation from digital teams and occasionally an upgrade project can be a significant effort. In this post we try to take some of the mystery out of a CMS upgrade, detailing the process we follow at 3chillies and answering some key questions.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ca href=\u0022/3chillies-blog/3chillies-umbraco-2024-codegarden-takeaways/\u0022 title=\u00223chillies Umbraco 2024 Codegarden Takeaways\u0022\u003E3chillies Umbraco 2024 Codegarden takeaways\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWe love Umbraco. It\u2019s one of our favourite platforms to work with. It\u2019s also had another busy year with acquisitions, new features, the release of Umbraco 14, changes at the top and more! We\u2019ve covered Umbraco a number of times on the blog, including in this post about some takeaways from the annual flagship Codegarden conference held in Denmark. We also covered Umbraco events in more detail recently, with an update that looked at everything from the release of Umbraco 15 to the launch of Umbraco Engage. Reviewing these posts will give you a flavour of what happened at Umbraco HQ in 2024, and a sense of what will happen in 2025.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ca href=\u0022/3chillies-blog/seven-tips-for-planning-your-website-or-digital-budget/\u0022 title=\u0022Seven tips for planning your website or digital budget\u0022\u003ESeven tips for planning your website budget\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EBudgeting for either a project or for the year can be a mixture of sophisticated data analysis, guesswork and the dark arts. You need to get it right to be accurate but ask for enough so that if it is challenged and subsequently cut, then your project is still viable. In this post from June, we looked at some of tips for planning a website budget that we\u2019ve learned over the years. These include partnering with your agency and \u2013 very importantly \u2013 never forgetting some contingency!\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ca href=\u0022/3chillies-blog/eight-ways-to-improve-the-environmental-impact-of-your-website/\u0022 title=\u0022Eight ways to improve the environmental impact of your website\u0022\u003EEight ways to improve the environmental impact of your website\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EBeing more sustainable and reaching Net Zero remain important goals for many organisations. Unfortunately, every website has a negative impact on the environment; but the good news is that there are lots that can be done to reduce this. In this important post we explore some of the ways that digital teams can reduce the carbon footprint of their website project and help contribute to sustainable goals. We\u2019re finding more and more of our clients want to achieve this \u2013 and the good news is that often-taking actions also reduces costs and often increases usability too.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ca href=\u0022/3chillies-blog/nine-components-of-a-successful-digital-agency-support-model/\u0022 title=\u0022Nine components of a successful digital agency support model\u0022\u003ENine components of a successful digital agency support model\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn some digital agencies, support is sometimes regarded as a secondary service compared to the development work that happens within a project. Yes, it\u2019s less glamorous but it\u2019s actually critical to the success and value of any website or app, and often shapes successful working relationships between agency and client. At 3chillies we are very proud of our support service and team, and we view it as just as important as our project work. In this post from August, we explore the successful components of a support model, including the \u201Cproactive maintenance\u201D which is at the centre of our proposition.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ca href=\u0022/3chillies-blog/ten-ways-to-fall-back-in-love-with-your-cms/\u0022 title=\u0022Ten ways to fall back in love with your CMS\u0022\u003ETen ways to fall back in love with your CMS\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIt\u2019s surprisingly easy to fall out of love with technology, especially if you have to use it every day and it has issues which cause frustration and waste time. Failing out of love with a Content Management System (CMS) is not uncommon for digital marketing teams and may eventually drive a re-platforming project. However, before you push the button there are multiple ways you can improve your CMS experience and even fall back in love with your CMS. In this post we cover ten things to consider to get your relationship with your CMS back on track.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ca href=\u0022/3chillies-blog/optimizely-update-why-the-platform-keeps-on-going-from-strength-to-strength/\u0022 title=\u0022Optimizely update: why the platform keeps on going from strength to strength\u0022\u003EOptimizely update: why the platform keeps on going from strength to strength\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOptimizely is a leading Digital Experience Platform (DXP) that we love to use here at 3chillies. It\u2019s also another platform that has had a very successful year. In this post from October, we reflected on the most significant recent updates from Optimizely, including announcements about revenue growth and a raft of new features and capabilities. We also explored the\u00A0rebranding of the product to Optimizely One and mentioned the release of Optimizely\u2019s CMS 13 in 2025. Next year looks set to be another exciting year for the DXP.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHere\u0027s to 2025!\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThanks very much for reading our blog during the year. We hope you\u2019ve found it interesting and useful, and we\u2019ll be back next year for another year of insights and observations, and a new revamped website too! Until then, we hope you have a wonderful Christmas and winter holiday season, as well as very happy and healthy new year. See you in 2025!\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E"
          }
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
